  "hypothesis": "V2-3h left most of both views' shimmer unattributed after window emission, ambient occlusion and the display transform were measured, and showed that finer sampling of the window light makes a near facade worse because the shipped analytic filters are already wider than a box filter. What no shader filter reaches is the geometry edge: the opaque scene is rendered into a single-sample buffer, so every silhouette is decided by one coverage test per pixel. Multisampling the opaque pass samples coverage four times per pixel while shading still runs once, which leaves every fwidth-derived filter at the width it has now and antialiases the edges alone.",
  "shipped_shader_sha256": {},
  "frames": 24,
  "pixels_per_frame_at_centre": 0.6180339887498949,
  "supersample": 4,
  "guard_scales_px": [
    4,
    16
  ],
  "rule": "Accepted only if, in every view, the candidate's display excess flicker is not higher than the shipped shader's by more than the tolerance, and it is no further from the supersampled reference at any guard scale by more than the tolerance; and its display excess flicker is lower by more than the tolerance in at least one view. The tolerance is the larger of the difference between two separate runs of the shipped shader and one percent of the shipped value.",
  "rule_revision": "V2-3g: excess flicker replaced the share of pixels over two code values, which saturated where fixes act (V2-3f); linear HDR is reported without deciding; a one percent equivalence margin was added; frame cost is not part of acceptance while performance work is deferred. All declared before any candidate was re-judged.",
